    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Hyundai Tucson N Line Steals the Spotlight in Paris, but It's All Show and No Go\nChinese Automaker Chery Looking Into Canadian Vehicle Assembly Plant\n7 photos\nPhoto: Chery\nLooking at the bigger picture, an idle Canadian auto plant makes a lot more sense to a Chinese automaker than a shiny new factory. The folks at Chery are currently looking at ways to assemble vehicles in Canada, with executive vice president Guibing Zhang confirming that existing production capacity is what Chery is after.\nSpeaking to Bloomberg, the VP also made it clear that partnerships with local investors are on the menu as well. While Chery has yet to name a partner or site, the idea presented by Zhang appears to be more than a vague intention to expand overseas. And truth be told, localization would make a world of difference over Chinese rivals that have no such ambitions in North America.\nChery will enter the Canadian market in 2027, likely with the Omoda and Jaecoo brands. Extremely popular in the Old Continent, said marques racked up a great deal of registrations in September 2026. For example, the Omoda 5 moved 8,396 units in the European market, putting it 43rd between the Skoda Enyaq and Kamiq. The Jaecoo 7 is 10th overall, having sold 15,470 units last month.\nStarting with imported vehicles in Canada would let Chery test demand for more affordable and well-equipped Chinese models before committing to manufacturing. Even so, Chery needs local partners to establish a proper dealership and servicing network. Canada has also changed the game for Chinese models, especially if we're talking about battery-electric models.\nBack in January, an agreement reached with Beijing created a quota system that allows manufacturers to bring in 49,000 Chinese electric vehicles under a most favored nation tariff of 6.1 percent. The quota system, which increases each year by 6.5 percent, replaces the additional 100 percent surtax from before. With increasingly more Chinese BEVs allowed on an annual basis, it goes without saying that Chinese brands are rubbing their hands now.\nPhoto: Chery Upgrading to a Canadian factory would give Chery a long-term presence, one that would involve Canadian suppliers and partners. Zhang's position is that Canada makes sense even if Chery never gets the okay to enter the US market, and he's on to something here. Trump has already shown openness to Chinese vehicle assembly plants in the United States, but only if they employ local workers.\nChery and plenty others are still looking for a way into the United States of America, partly because it's far more lucrative than Canada. As far as we know, Chery remains open to partnerships with American automakers. The one that comes to mind is Stellantis, whose European division already builds Chinese models for the Leapmotor brand in Spain .\nOttawa has already encouraged discussions with a variety of Chinese automakers, including Chery. It, BYD, and Geely are all interested in exploring joint ventures on Canadian soil. Executive vice president Guibing Zhang indicated that Chery would also consider investment from multiple parties rather than financing the whole Canadian operation itself.\nRegardless of option, every single one of them would bring its own commitments and costs. An idled plant avoids the expense and lead time of constructing a new one, but Chery would still have to make everything fit its intended vehicles and the market as a whole.\nBYD, meanwhile, has already inquired about the idled Stellantis plant in Brampton, Ontario. Geely is also starting Canadian sales in 2027, but as opposed to BYD and Chery, it still hasn't announced whether local production may be in the offing.\nVia // Bloomberg\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChery Canada China industry production\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Would You Make Your BMW X6 M Look Like This?",
